5:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-20","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-muted/30 border border-border/40 rounded-3xl p-10 md:p-16","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-4 text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-10","children":[["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-book-open text-primary w-10 h-10","children":[["$","path","1akyts",{"d":"M12 7v14"}],["$","path","ruj8y",{"d":"M3 18a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 4 4 0 0 1 4-4h5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-6a3 3 0 0 0-3 3 3 3 0 0 0-3-3z"}],"$undefined"]}],"Deep Understanding"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":"Classical Samkhya philosophy radically splits reality into exactly two components. Everything you can touch, see, feel, remember, or think about is Prakriti (Matter/Nature). The one thing you cannot physically grasp is the thing doing the grasping: Purusha. The Purusha is pure consciousness. It has no personality, no desires, no memory, and no anxiety. It simply watches. Human suffering begins the moment this flawless, untouched observer confuses itself with the turbulent, changing machinery of the mind and body (Prakriti). Liberation is simply the Purusha remembering that it is only watching the movie, not starring in it."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"italic border-l-2 border-primary/20 pl-6 py-2","children":"The foundational dualism of Samkhya and Patanjali's Yoga Sutras. Without the concept of an unmoving observer (Purusha), the entire practice of quieting the mind (Chitta Vritti Nirodha) has no ultimate destination."}]]}]]}]}]
